  ('ev_wiki_chief-joseph_1877_13', 'ent_chief-joseph', NULL, 1877, 'personal_life', 'narrative',
   'McWhorter interviewed and befriended Nez Perce warriors such as Yellow Wolf, who stated, "Our hearts have always been in the valley of the Wallowa".[15]  Robert Forczyk states in his book Nez Perce 18', 'McWhorter interviewed and befriended Nez Perce warriors such as Yellow Wolf, who stated, "Our hearts have always been in the valley of the Wallowa".[15]  Robert Forczyk states in his book Nez Perce 1877: The Last Fight that the tipping point of the war was that "Joseph responded that his clan''s traditions would not allow him to cede the Wallowa Valley".[16] The band led by Chief Joseph never signed the treaty moving them to the Idaho reservation.', 'src_en_wikipedia',
