   'We set the thing up front in D-flat, and then we just went on playing in F." It became his signature tune.[28]  John Hammond and first recordings  At the end of 1936, Basie and his band, now billed as', 'We set the thing up front in D-flat, and then we just went on playing in F." It became his signature tune.[28]  John Hammond and first recordings  At the end of 1936, Basie and his band, now billed as Count Basie and His Barons of Rhythm, moved from Kansas City to Chicago, where they honed their repertoire at a long engagement at the Grand Terrace Cafe.[29] Right from the start, Basie''s band was known for its rhythm section.', 'src_en_wikipedia',
